## Fault matrix — outcome per fault per variant

| Fault | baseline | durable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `kill-mid-turn` | **lost** | **resumed** |
| `restart-after-checkpoint` | **lost** | **resumed** |
| `duplicate-delivery` | **doubled** | **doubled** |
| `concurrent-clients` | **not corrupted** | **not corrupted** |
| `store-outage` | **not exercised** | **failed safely, with a split store** |

## What each result observed

### `kill-mid-turn`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **lost** | a model turn in flight (a tool result held by the gate, the answer not yet rece… | The enquiry was mid-turn when pid 74779 was killed. A new process, same port, same working directory, shows 0 cards: processing 0, completed 0, rejected 0. Nothing resumes: the agent lived in a Dictionary<string, Agent> that died with the process, and the SDK session id was never retained. |
| durable | **resumed** | committed at validating when the process was killed | The kill left a committed document at phase validating with 2 entries and task status running. A new process resumed 1 task(s) at phase validating and finished with task terminal at phase done, 10 entries, restarts=1, report 46 characters. The turn that was cut off was re-sent, not replayed: it left no answer to reuse. |

### `restart-after-checkpoint`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **lost** | a committed phase, process stopped while waiting on the runtime | The server was stopped cleanly (a committed phase, process stopped while waiting on the runtime) and restarted. It shows 0 cards: processing 0, completed 0. Whatever phase had committed is gone with the process, so there is no resume to be exact about. |
| durable | **resumed** | committed at validating when the process was stopped (SIGTERM while waiting on … | The restart resumed 1 task(s) from the committed phase and finished at done with 10 entries (task terminal). The committed checkpoint was ahead of the last completed turn, so the remaining work ran and the finished part did not. |

### `duplicate-delivery`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **doubled** | the same enquiry delivered twice | Two deliveries of the same enquiry produced 2 agents on the page (Need a downtown condo with parking, 1-2 bedrooms, under $350k \| Need a downtown condo with parking, 1-2 bedrooms, under $350k) and 2 model turns reached the runtime. The application has no identity for an enquiry, so the second delivery is a second agent: its side effects — a Copilot session, a search, a report — happen twice. |
| durable | **doubled** | the same enquiry delivered twice | Two deliveries of the same enquiry produced 2 enquiry documents, 2 model admissions and 2 search tool call(s), across 18 transcript entries. The variant has no identity for an enquiry to deduplicate on, so the second delivery is a second conversation: the search and the report happen twice, and the store keeps both documents intact — nothing was corrupted, but nothing was reused either. |

### `concurrent-clients`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **not corrupted** | two enquiries in flight together | Both enquiries completed with the first held inside a tool call when the second was submitted: 2 agents on the page, completed=2, rejected=0, 2 model turn(s) reached the runtime. In this sample the dictionary race did not manifest — the defect is present in the code (unlocked Dictionary<string, Agent> mutated from every run task), but one concurrent pair is not enough to make it throw. This row is a tie on behaviour and a difference only in the code. |
| durable | **not corrupted** | two enquiries in flight together | 2 enquiry documents and 2 task records, all 2 terminal, 16 transcript entries and 0 entries crossing documents. The commit gate serialized the two writers, so neither document saw the other's entries. |

### `store-outage`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **not exercised** | a store that holds agent state | The baseline keeps every agent in an in-process Dictionary<string, Agent> and writes no agent state to disk for the lifetime of the process. There is no store file to remove, lock, or corrupt: properties.db holds property listings only, and an outage of it would test the search tool rather than the durability of an enquiry. Removing it mid-run was not substituted, because a different fault reported under this name would be worse than an honest absence. |
| durable | **failed safely, with a split store** | a model turn in flight, store removed mid-write | The store was moved aside mid-turn. The application did not fail: it wrote a new file at the same path, so the live path was recreated and now holds phase done with 9 entries, in 51 records, while the moved file still holds the committed prefix intact (28 records, 0 torn, phase validating, 2 entries, phase; intent:Validate home enquiry). Nothing was lost, but the state is now in two files: an operator reading the live path sees a different enquiry history from the one the process held a moment before the outage, and restoring the moved file would be a second store rather than a merge. The application fails safe in the sense that it reports no corruption and keeps writing; it does not fail loudly, and it does not tell anyone the old file was gone. |

